Z||000000||starfighter007@msn.com||07-29-2001||04:21 PM||||Hey buddy I just thought of something really simple so simple that I cant believe that some one has not thought of this yet.and maybe for good reason also .but I was thinking about the trouble a lot of people are getting into bleeding brakes and clutches .do you think it would be possible to get a pump up bug spayer change the hose on it and make a connetion or simple coupler for M/C.cap or clutch cap .fill the spay bottle up with brake fluid ,and then walk around to each bleeder  and start bleeding away.If this would be possible this has got to be a inexpensive way to go to bleed our hydrolic system on our cars?Would love to to here your thughts on this. ||||reg||3
Z||000001||KissMySSFiero||07-29-2001||11:05 PM||||great idea.  but its been done.  i know for gm its one of theyre reguired tools at a dealership(J tools)  the only thing is its still easier for a mechanic to just ask someone to hop in the car when its up on the lift rather than diggin up the tool from the back room.  however when your by yourself the thing is the best thing you could imagine.<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||07-29-2001||11:40 PM||||It's called a power bleeder.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-29-2001||11:50 PM||||There are all sorts of pressure bleeding systems on the market. reliable and safe ones cost a good chunk of change. You can't just dump brake fluid into a tank and pump air over the top of it. If you do you'll have brake fluid with allot of water in it... not good.<P>I do have something in the works for "pressure bleeding" the MC using a special cap. You can't just pump air into the cap either. same problem as a big tank... wet air and brake fluid don't mix. Also you can't do this with anything but a small hand pump, very small, or the cap will blow off. (Inotherwords, No, you can't just punch a hole in the cap and pressurize the thing...)<P>I want to finish fooling with it and test it before I publish anything.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric_PA||07-30-2001||05:50 AM||||I have to jump in here quick and mention that there are Russel Speed Bleeders available from Summit Racing.... They work great and cost $10 for two... I'm not at home right now to get the part numbers... You just crack them open a bit and pump the pedal as much as your heart desires. No need for a second person.. I use them on all of my cars now.<P>Eric||||reg||6
Z||000005||Will||07-30-2001||10:23 AM||||I've been using a power bleeder for some time now.<BR>I've modified a Fiero brake master cylinder (and clutch master cylinder) cap by drilling a hole and putting in a quick release fitting. Since I already had an air compressor and a low pressure regulator, pressurizing the master cylinder with 6 psi was not a problem.<BR>My dad has been doing this same thing to his car for <I>years</I> without taking any special precautions against getting water in the fluid. It's really a non-issue considering the tiny amount of air which actually contacts the fluid.||||reg||1
Z||000006||starfighter007@msn.com||07-30-2001||10:00 PM||||well I knew that this was to easy of a tool.I guess that moistier thing would be a big problem.that was something I didnot thik about.It sure would be nice to have something that is kinda  easy but affordable.but if we dont ask we will never know.I am still very intrested in somthing to flush out brakeM/C and clutchM/C.I am sure Iam not the only one.thank you all for your wisdom on this subject.||||reg||11
